+This study develops a two-stage joint scheduling model for PECVD rotating-chamber cluster tools. Wafer pairing connects product and auxiliary supply/return routes, while chamber assignment, resource order, physical-hand assignment, and event times jointly represent full occupancy, auxiliary reuse, periodic cleaning, and residency constraints. Single- and dual-source configurations differ in the origin and route of auxiliary wafers, and clean/dirty-hand eligibility applies throughout each holding interval.
+
+All 107 main runs and seven small-instance structural checks of the clean/dirty-hand model produce independently validated physical schedules. For four products, reassigning auxiliary identities and reoptimizing timing while preserving product pairing, chamber assignments, cycle structure, and the eight-auxiliary inventory reduces makespan from the original fixed-structure optimum of 532~s to 403~s, a reduction of 24.25\%, with 111~s of concurrent chamber production. Auxiliary-loan relationships are thus substantive structural decisions affecting attainable concurrency and makespan. Across six single- and dual-source configurations under the same 300-s total solve budget, two-stage refinement reduces total transport-route waiting by 8.40\%--29.21\%, decreases maximum individual waiting from 73 to 65~s, and shortens actual product makespan in every case. The 72 mode-sweep cells for lots of 8, 16, and 24 wafers characterize feasible schedules under each cleaning condition; pure $4\times1$ gives the shortest observed makespan for 24 wafers throughout the tested $H=3$--10 range. Eight small source-route schedules additionally realize product--auxiliary pairing, cross-LP pairing of same-recipe products, and both supply/return routes with valid clean/dirty-hand assignments.
